/*****************************************************************************
    kantRuleS -- THE H7 NEGATIVE CONTROL FOR kantRuleA. 2026-08-11.

    kantRuleA is green on all four rows. A green certifies only what fails
    when the mechanism is removed, and here the mechanism is short circuit --
    and removing it costs nothing at all, because the SYMBOL forms are the
    EAGER spelling of the same two operators on one shared handler. So this
    file is kantRuleA with the alternation's OR changed to the bar pair, and
    nothing else changed.

    WHAT IT SHOWS, and it is a CURSOR result, not a tick result. Measured:

        row              kantRuleA (OR)        this file (bar pair)
        1 first fails    ticks 1  cursor 0     ticks 1  cursor 0
        2 all pass       ticks 3  cursor 3     ticks 4  CURSOR 4
        4 alt opt 1 ok   ticks 3, opt 2 SKIPPED ticks 2  CURSOR 2

    ⚠ THE EAGER DISJUNCTION CONSUMES A TOKEN THAT NO OPTION MATCHED. Option
    one succeeds and option two runs anyway and advances the cursor, so the
    rule reports success having eaten one more token than it matched. That is
    precisely the harm the 08-08 assessment refused the AND spelling over --
    a parse term consumes input, so an eagerly evaluated arm advances the mark
    past text the rule never matched -- and it is here, measured, on a rule
    shape rather than in the abstract.

    ⚠⚠ A VERDICT ONLY CONTROL WOULD HAVE SEEN NOTHING. Both spellings return
    SUCCESS on rows 2 and 4. They differ only in how much input they ate, and
    only the cursor tells them apart. A harness asserting the rule's verdict
    would have certified the eager spelling as correct.

    ⚠⚠ AND THE CONJUNCTION IS NOT IN THIS FILE, WHICH IS ITSELF THE FINDING.
    The symbol conjunction over CALLS does not answer wrongly -- it KILLS THE
    PARSE: exit 139, ZERO bytes of output, before the search list line, so it
    reads as a broken binary rather than a broken spelling. Bisected the same
    day, one operator at a time, on this exact file: the AND chain with the
    bar pair runs clean and exits 0, while the ampersand pair with the word OR
    dies at 139 with no output. So the eager conjunction cannot appear in a
    control because a fixture that dies before its first row is not a control,
    it is an absence -- and it would cost completePop its zero missing
    sentinels baseline. Independently reproduced on plain field operands, no
    calls, under the jit: take incant jitXand2 and change the word to the
    ampersand pair. Full record and the repair consequence: docs knownErrors
    KE-5's 2026-08-11 amendment.

    INTERPRETED ARM ONLY. With no testing call there is no isCoded routing to
    protect, so bear-trap 25 does not apply and the rows simply run.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(KantRuleS);
define
    ksPos;
    ksTicks;
    ksSuk;
    ksOut;
    ksOK1;
    ksOK2;
    ksOK3;
    ksAltOK1;

    /*  A TERM. Advances the cursor, and on failure puts it back itself --
        the mark-restore-inside-the-term convention the AND chain depends on
        for soundness. A term that did not do this could not be short
        circuited safely, which is the whole reason the convention exists.  */
    ksT1 code={
        ksTicks = ksTicks + 1;
        ksPos = ksPos + 1;
        if ksOK1 == 0;
            ksPos = ksPos - 1;
            return 0;
        return 1;
        };
    ksT2 code={
        ksTicks = ksTicks + 1;
        ksPos = ksPos + 1;
        if ksOK2 == 0;
            ksPos = ksPos - 1;
            return 0;
        return 1;
        };
    ksT3 code={
        ksTicks = ksTicks + 1;
        ksPos = ksPos + 1;
        if ksOK3 == 0;
            ksPos = ksPos - 1;
            return 0;
        return 1;
        };

    /*  THE ALTERNATION'S TWO OPTIONS. Same contract. The second one always
        succeeds, so row 3 can show that a failed first option costs nothing
        in cursor terms.  */
    ksA1 code={
        ksTicks = ksTicks + 1;
        ksPos = ksPos + 1;
        if ksAltOK1 == 0;
            ksPos = ksPos - 1;
            return 0;
        return 1;
        };
    ksA2 code={
        ksTicks = ksTicks + 1;
        ksPos = ksPos + 1;
        return 1;
        };

    /*  THE ALTERNATION, an OR chain in its own method -- which is how a
        generated rule would emit an option list.  */
    ksAlt code={
        return ksA1() || ksA2();
        };

    /*  THE RULE. Three terms, the middle one an alternation. This is the
        whole method: one statement where the if chain needs seven.  */
    ksRule code={
        ksSuk = ksT1() AND ksAlt() AND ksT3();
        if ksSuk == 0;
            return 0;
        return 1;
        };
    ;

/*  ROW 1 -- INTERPRETED. First term fails. Under the word forms this gave
    ticks 1 and cursor 0. Anything else here is the control firing.  */
ksOK1 = 0; ksOK2 = 1; ksOK3 = 1; ksAltOK1 = 1;
ksPos = 0; ksTicks = 0; ksOut = 9;
print "KS row 1 setup  : first term FAILS, cursor at" ksPos:;
ksRule();
print "KS row 1 result : ticks =" ksTicks " cursor =" ksPos:;
print "KS row 1 verdict: kantRuleA gave ticks 1 cursor 0 -- any other pair here is OVER-CONSUMPTION":;

/*  ROW 2 -- INTERPRETED. Everything passes.  */
ksOK1 = 1; ksOK2 = 1; ksOK3 = 1; ksAltOK1 = 1;
ksPos = 0; ksTicks = 0;
print "KS row 2 setup  : all terms PASS":;
ksRule();
print "KS row 2 result : ticks =" ksTicks " cursor =" ksPos:;

/*  ROW 4 -- INTERPRETED. The alternation's first option passes, so under a
    short-circuiting OR the second must never run.  */
ksOK1 = 1; ksOK2 = 1; ksOK3 = 1; ksAltOK1 = 1;
ksPos = 0; ksTicks = 0;
print "KS row 4 setup  : alternation option 1 PASSES":;
ksAlt();
print "KS row 4 result : ticks =" ksTicks " cursor =" ksPos:;
print "KS row 4 verdict: ticks 1 = the bar pair short-circuits, 2 = it is eager":;

print "KS SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
